# The bundled DeepSeek tokenizer assets are byte-exact: their SHA-256 is
# verified at load time and any line-ending conversion breaks that check.
# Force binary (LF-preserving) checkout for every asset under the tokenizer
# directories so a Windows `core.autocrlf=true` checkout cannot corrupt them.
#
# The license files are covered too: the manifests hash them alongside the
# tokenizer, and a CRLF checkout of a `.txt` license inflates it by exactly one
# byte per line, which surfaces as `byte length differs from manifest` in
# `verify:release`.
packages/runtime/assets/** -text

# Build output ships through git (see .gitignore): a `pnpm add git+https://...`
# install never runs `prepack`, so it consumes `lib/` exactly as committed. The
# artifacts are generated with LF, so pin LF on checkout as well — otherwise a
# Windows `core.autocrlf=true` clone rewrites every artifact and the committed
# bytes stop matching what the build produced.
packages/*/lib/** -text

# The offline install package carries the same byte-exact tokenizer assets and
# a distribution snapshot of the vendored JavaScript. Without this rule a
# Windows `core.autocrlf=true` clone rewrites every vendored file on checkout,
# and the installer then copies a CRLF tokenizer.json into the profile whose
# SHA-256 no longer matches — which surfaces as a silent fail-open (no exact
# counting, so no compression at all) rather than an obvious error.
release-package/vendor/** -text
